        summary = "Lending stays mixed: member-owned credit bodies, public savings banks and private banks lend under one statewide rulebook. Deposit-taking stays apart from securities trading, holding companies answer to the central bank, and losses from bank rescues fall on shareholders and creditors before any public money.",
        details = "Keeps: a barrier between commercial and investment banking (Keynesian welfare capitalism; US Glass-Steagall Act of 1933 and its repeal in 1999); central bank oversight of holding companies (Monetarist neoliberalism); member control of lenders (Mutualism; Alphonse Desjardins and the caisse populaire in Lévis, Quebec); a public authority for bank aid (Nordic social democracy; Sweden's bank rescue of 1991 to 1993). Blocks: government approval needed to found a bank, safeguarded by licences on published tests (Social market economy); public money to rescue lenders at a net cost, safeguarded by a capped fund, with shareholders losing first (Sweden's bank rescue of 1991 to 1993); debt repaid through labour, safeguarded by a ban on such repayment (Gift economy; Feudalism). Test: over ten years, reviewed biennially, measure small loans made without collateral, arrears, refusals and appeals, and the net public cost of each rescue, with its method. Source: Congressional Research Service report R44349; Desjardins's own history timeline; Journal of Financial Crises case study; DSGV history document; Mauss, The Gift; Bloch, Feudal Society.",
